1
00:00:00,691 --> 00:00:02,383
Sync and corrections by n17t01
www.addic7ed.com

2
00:00:04,486 --> 00:00:05,793
<i>The first day</i>
<i>of school--</i>

3
00:00:05,934 --> 00:00:08,169
<i>a day filled</i>
<i>with unbridled optimism</i>

4
00:00:08,266 --> 00:00:09,375
<i>for the year ahead.</i>

5
00:00:09,528 --> 00:00:12,095
Aah! It's the first day
of school! I'm so excited!

6
00:00:12,183 --> 00:00:15,516
<i>Then again, that describes</i>
<i>just about every day for Sue.</i>

7
00:00:15,698 --> 00:00:17,289
Braided my hair.

8
00:00:19,334 --> 00:00:22,617
My nose stopped bleeding!
I'm so excited!

9
00:00:22,989 --> 00:00:24,065
Finished my apple.

10
00:00:24,166 --> 00:00:25,400
I'm so excited.

11
00:00:26,492 --> 00:00:28,923
This is going to be
the best year ever,

12
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:30,502
because now that
I'm a soph--

13
00:00:30,589 --> 00:00:31,754
that's short
for "sophomore"--

14
00:00:31,835 --> 00:00:33,971
I get to be a mentor
to an incoming freshman.

15
00:00:34,088 --> 00:00:35,503
It's an amazing program.

16
00:00:35,609 --> 00:00:37,615
It really keeps newbies
from falling through the cracks.

17
00:00:37,719 --> 00:00:40,678
Sue, nobody cares about your
stupid suck-more activities.

18
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:42,763
This year
is all about the seniors.

19
00:00:42,870 --> 00:00:44,197
Seniors rule!

20
00:00:44,286 --> 00:00:45,615
Is he gonna be doing that
all year?

21
00:00:45,730 --> 00:00:47,961
Well, anybody wants a ride,
let's go.

22
00:00:48,059 --> 00:00:49,454
Don't forget--
I'm working a double shift,

23
00:00:49,561 --> 00:00:51,023
so you're on your own
for dinner.

24
00:00:51,116 --> 00:00:51,958
And if you can get to it,

25
00:00:52,068 --> 00:00:54,606
there's old Thanksgiving turkey
in the back of the freezer.

26
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:55,840
Hey, Brick, after school,

27
00:00:55,925 --> 00:00:57,409
you're coming to
my softball game with me.

28
00:00:57,502 --> 00:01:00,170
Softball game? Why don't you
just take me to the beach?

29
00:01:05,789 --> 00:01:07,024
Hi. I'm looking for--

30
00:01:07,115 --> 00:01:08,070
Sue Heck?

31
00:01:08,174 --> 00:01:10,314
Well, look no further,
'cause you found her.

32
00:01:10,423 --> 00:01:11,386
I'm your mentor!

33
00:01:11,527 --> 00:01:13,430
- Aah!
- Hey.

34
00:01:13,599 --> 00:01:15,005
Great to meet you.

35
00:01:15,132 --> 00:01:15,916
Jenna Taylor.

36
00:01:16,011 --> 00:01:17,103
I know...

37
00:01:17,201 --> 00:01:21,906
Which is why I "Taylor"-made
this binder for you.

38
00:01:22,019 --> 00:01:25,397
"Sue's tips for Sue-cess."

39
00:01:25,534 --> 00:01:26,700
Sit.

40
00:01:27,747 --> 00:01:29,825
This is chockfull
of inside scoop

41
00:01:29,931 --> 00:01:31,464
to help you get through
your freshman year.

42
00:01:31,603 --> 00:01:33,926
Notice I did not call it
"frosh" year.

43
00:01:34,065 --> 00:01:37,015
Nobody calls it "frosh."
I learned that one the hard way.

44
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:38,667
So how about we dive right in?

45
00:01:38,919 --> 00:01:40,097
We have...

46
00:01:40,193 --> 00:01:41,259
Stuff to try out for,

47
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:43,185
locker room dos and don'ts,

48
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:46,945
funny things to say when
you fall down the stairs.

49
00:01:47,068 --> 00:01:49,798
Wow. There lot more to try
out for than in middle school.

50
00:01:49,914 --> 00:01:50,908
Pretty exciting.

51
00:01:51,012 --> 00:01:52,984
It's <i>incredibly</i> exciting.

52
00:01:53,274 --> 00:01:54,973
Don't expect to make anything.

53
00:01:55,078 --> 00:01:56,939
As a freshman,
that's off the table.

54
00:01:57,036 --> 00:01:59,193
But it's still good to try
so you can, you know,

55
00:01:59,359 --> 00:02:00,573
get your face out there.

56
00:02:00,675 --> 00:02:02,674
Oh! And this is
for you to keep.

57
00:02:02,815 --> 00:02:03,884
Wow.

58
00:02:04,046 --> 00:02:06,079
Lucky my mom bought me
the big backpack.

59
00:02:06,270 --> 00:02:08,798
Now we should probably talk
at least three times a day.

60
00:02:09,387 --> 00:02:12,177
This is my cell.
If I don't pick up, don't worry.

61
00:02:12,283 --> 00:02:13,778
My battery charger
is on the fritz,

62
00:02:13,868 --> 00:02:15,594
and they discontinued it
and the phone, like,

63
00:02:15,689 --> 00:02:16,551
11 years ago.

64
00:02:16,664 --> 00:02:19,127
But if you're ever in a bind
and you can't get ahold of me,

65
00:02:19,252 --> 00:02:23,849
kickinitteenstyle.com
is an <i>excellent</i> resource

66
00:02:23,967 --> 00:02:26,638
for all of kinds of advice.
It's kinda my Bible.

67
00:02:26,747 --> 00:02:28,175
Oh, my gosh.
I just realized you must have

68
00:02:28,286 --> 00:02:29,706
a ton of questions for me.

69
00:02:29,982 --> 00:02:31,190
Actually, I do have one.

70
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:32,158
Bring it.

71
00:02:32,267 --> 00:02:34,438
What time does
the late bus run until?

72
00:02:35,136 --> 00:02:36,477
I do not know that,

73
00:02:36,594 --> 00:02:39,454
<i>but</i> I-I can find out
and get back to you in a jiff.

74
00:02:43,904 --> 00:02:45,578
Uhh!

75
00:02:49,602 --> 00:02:50,727
F.Y.I.,

76
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:54,196
they mop the cafeteria hallway
every morning around this time.

77
00:02:54,585 --> 00:02:56,454
Can we get an orange cone
over here?

78
00:02:57,705 --> 00:03:00,176
<i>While Sue was lying down the rules</i>
<i>of high school survival,</i>

79
00:03:00,325 --> 00:03:03,340
<i>I was all too versed in</i>
<i>the rules of Ehlert survival--</i>

80
00:03:03,519 --> 00:03:05,726
<i>hide the sprinkled doughnuts</i>
<i>in your purse for later,</i>

81
00:03:05,847 --> 00:03:07,393
<i>shuffle papers</i>
<i>so you look busy,</i>

82
00:03:07,532 --> 00:03:08,733
<i>and know that the first thing</i>

83
00:03:09,135 --> 00:03:10,422
<i>out of Ehlert's mouth</i>
<i>when he see you</i>

84
00:03:10,591 --> 00:03:12,089
<i>will always be an insult.</i>

85
00:03:12,196 --> 00:03:13,457
Afternoon, Frances.

86
00:03:13,586 --> 00:03:14,688
Mm.

87
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:16,030
You look nice today.

88
00:03:16,178 --> 00:03:17,175
Oh.

89
00:03:17,801 --> 00:03:19,036
Th-thank you?

90
00:03:19,149 --> 00:03:20,544
See you in my office?

91
00:03:26,616 --> 00:03:28,437
Hey, think about sliding
next time.

92
00:03:28,613 --> 00:03:29,655
Hey, Mike.

93
00:03:29,824 --> 00:03:32,303
I don't know if you know my son.
Brian, say "hello" to Mr. Heck.

94
00:03:32,410 --> 00:03:34,127
Hi, Mr. Heck.
Nice to meet you.

95
00:03:34,234 --> 00:03:36,092
Nice to meet you, too, Brian.
This is my son Brick.

96
00:03:36,201 --> 00:03:37,834
Brick, say "hello"
to the Morrisons.

97
00:03:39,939 --> 00:03:41,723
I said,
say "hello" to the Morrisons.

98
00:03:41,832 --> 00:03:42,985
I'm reading.

99
00:03:43,805 --> 00:03:46,671
And I'm waiting for you to
say "hello" to the Morrisons.

100
00:03:46,848 --> 00:03:49,383
Fine. Hello.

101
00:03:49,537 --> 00:03:50,588
Happy?

102
00:03:50,703 --> 00:03:53,022
Whoa. You'd kill me
if I talked to you like that.

103
00:03:53,132 --> 00:03:54,426
You bet I would.

104
00:03:54,616 --> 00:03:56,369
Don't worry.
I'll be killing him later.

105
00:04:00,112 --> 00:04:01,417
Here's the thing, Frances.

106
00:04:01,589 --> 00:04:04,411
The truth is,
you don't look nice today.

107
00:04:05,355 --> 00:04:07,870
And since there's
no real way to say this,

108
00:04:07,967 --> 00:04:09,376
I'm just gonna say it.

109
00:04:09,910 --> 00:04:11,773
I'm gonna have to let you go.

110
00:04:12,456 --> 00:04:15,470
Let me go...
unless I sell more cars?

111
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:16,834
Let me rephrase that.

112
00:04:17,146 --> 00:04:18,681
I'm letting you go.

113
00:04:19,024 --> 00:04:21,440
Unless I start being
more on time?

114
00:04:21,524 --> 00:04:24,138
Frances, don't make this harder
on me than it is.

115
00:04:24,292 --> 00:04:27,320
I just can't afford to carry
this many people

116
00:04:27,442 --> 00:04:28,872
when business is slow.

117
00:04:29,005 --> 00:04:31,095
It's not you.
It's the economy.

118
00:04:31,223 --> 00:04:33,406
Oh, is this because I dozed off
in the back of the minivan?

119
00:04:33,523 --> 00:04:35,360
I'm gettin' that checked out.

120
00:04:35,496 --> 00:04:37,822
It might have something to do
with my iron levels.

121
00:04:37,931 --> 00:04:39,681
I don't want to hear
about any lady parts!

122
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:43,033
Look, this is all based
on seniority.

123
00:04:43,149 --> 00:04:44,769
Don't get me wrong.
You're plenty old.

124
00:04:44,889 --> 00:04:46,947
But you were
the last one hired.

125
00:04:47,077 --> 00:04:49,745
Wait. What?

126
00:04:49,955 --> 00:04:51,293
This is real?

127
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:53,294
You're really firing me?

128
00:04:53,415 --> 00:04:56,372
You can't do that.
My son's going to college.

129
00:04:56,505 --> 00:04:59,447
We just found out Sue's gonna
have a palate expander.

130
00:04:59,552 --> 00:05:00,387
Look, Frances,

131
00:05:00,502 --> 00:05:02,595
if the economy picks up
and things change,

132
00:05:02,742 --> 00:05:06,353
feel free to come back
and buy a car.

133
00:05:08,963 --> 00:05:10,832
<i>I just had to get out of there</i>
<i>without crying,</i>

134
00:05:10,959 --> 00:05:13,980
<i>because you don't want to be</i>
<i>the woman who loses it at work.</i>

135
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:17,416
Well, I know what
you're all thinking--

136
00:05:17,546 --> 00:05:19,968
Frankie get another promotion!
Ha ha ha ha!

137
00:05:20,083 --> 00:05:22,062
N-no. I-I got canned.

138
00:05:22,216 --> 00:05:24,122
But, hey, on the plus side,

139
00:05:24,268 --> 00:05:26,738
I was thinking about knocking
off early anyway. Ha ha ha!

140
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:32,750
This can't be happening.
It is total bull-roar.

141
00:05:32,871 --> 00:05:35,733
It's okay, Bob.
We'll still see each other.

142
00:05:35,842 --> 00:05:36,964
Okay.

143
00:05:40,950 --> 00:05:43,628
Oh, Frankie! Uh, I just heard
the news. I can't believe it.

144
00:05:44,712 --> 00:05:45,959
You knew, didn't you?

145
00:05:46,068 --> 00:05:47,054
Weeks ago.

146
00:05:47,141 --> 00:05:49,065
Well...
Doesn't matter, Pete,

147
00:05:49,156 --> 00:05:50,563
'cause I'm gonna be okay.

148
00:05:50,936 --> 00:05:52,366
You know what they say--

149
00:05:52,686 --> 00:05:56,111
when one door closes,
another one opens.

150
00:05:58,993 --> 00:06:00,647
Can somebody open this door
for me?

151
00:06:01,631 --> 00:06:03,145
Thanks, Bob.

152
00:06:04,251 --> 00:06:07,094
Well...
This really wasn't

153
00:06:07,192 --> 00:06:09,609
such a horrible place
to come to every day...

154
00:06:11,181 --> 00:06:14,144
and it's because of you guys.

155
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:16,692
Bye.

156
00:06:18,936 --> 00:06:20,766
Dibs on her desk.

157
00:06:21,692 --> 00:06:22,993
<i>Well, I did it.</i>

158
00:06:23,196 --> 00:06:24,335
<i>It was hard,</i>

159
00:06:24,463 --> 00:06:27,223
<i>but I got out of there</i>
<i>with my head held high.</i>

160
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:29,869
<i>Frankie, we're gonna miss you.</i>

161
00:06:33,339 --> 00:06:36,533
<i>And you left your purse</i>
<i>on your chair.</i>

162
00:06:36,652 --> 00:06:38,030
Oh, crap.

163
00:06:46,683 --> 00:06:49,627
I don't get it. Fired unless
you sell more cars?

164
00:06:49,743 --> 00:06:51,289
No. <i>Fired.</i>

165
00:06:51,417 --> 00:06:53,343
As in, he's gonna cut back
your hours?

166
00:06:53,459 --> 00:06:54,886
Yes, Mike, he's gonna
cut back my hours.

167
00:06:54,994 --> 00:06:56,684
He's gonna cut back
<i>all</i> my hours.

168
00:06:56,793 --> 00:06:58,273
How are you not getting this?

169
00:06:58,609 --> 00:06:59,786
Wow.

170
00:07:00,571 --> 00:07:02,708
So Ehlert really fired you.

171
00:07:04,483 --> 00:07:05,828
Okay.

172
00:07:06,696 --> 00:07:08,498
So you're fired.

173
00:07:09,078 --> 00:07:12,298
We've taken hits before.
We'll get through this.

174
00:07:14,624 --> 00:07:15,745
Mm.

175
00:07:15,849 --> 00:07:17,117
On the plus side,

176
00:07:17,454 --> 00:07:19,944
I didn't make that much money
to begin with.

177
00:07:20,213 --> 00:07:21,777
No, you did not.

178
00:07:25,451 --> 00:07:27,209
Ah! Jenna, there you are.

179
00:07:27,339 --> 00:07:29,547
I have an answer for you
on that late bus question.

180
00:07:29,696 --> 00:07:31,374
Oh! I forgot to tell you.
I was looking at

181
00:07:31,501 --> 00:07:32,858
all the different things
to try out for,

182
00:07:32,999 --> 00:07:35,686
and I decided to go
for cheerleading, and...

183
00:07:35,875 --> 00:07:37,337
I made it!

184
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:38,324
Wow!

185
00:07:38,473 --> 00:07:40,768
Are you <i>sure</i> you made it?

186
00:07:40,884 --> 00:07:42,924
Because sometimes they say
you made it,

187
00:07:43,051 --> 00:07:44,465
and then you tell everyone
the good news,

188
00:07:44,591 --> 00:07:46,710
including your pen pals
in Brazil and Toronto,

189
00:07:46,855 --> 00:07:48,337
but then they call you down
to the gym

190
00:07:48,446 --> 00:07:50,396
to tell you that
you <i>didn't</i> make it.

191
00:07:50,522 --> 00:07:52,138
Hmm.
I'm pretty sure I made it.

192
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:54,724
- They gave me a uniform
and everything. - They did?

193
00:07:57,216 --> 00:07:58,764
Oh, my God. Look at that!

194
00:07:58,868 --> 00:08:01,108
You're a cheerleader.
I'm a wrestlerette.

195
00:08:01,218 --> 00:08:03,277
It's funny how they matched us
up so perfectly.

196
00:08:03,393 --> 00:08:05,043
Do you wanna have lunch together
today to celebrate?

197
00:08:05,157 --> 00:08:06,354
- Sure.
- Great.

198
00:08:06,483 --> 00:08:08,141
I'll meet you
in Ms. Spivey's room at 1:00.

199
00:08:11,993 --> 00:08:13,721
Hey.

200
00:08:13,822 --> 00:08:14,844
Hey. How was work?

201
00:08:14,955 --> 00:08:17,031
Not bad.
How was... not working?

202
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:19,156
Well, I've been doing laundry
for two hours.

203
00:08:19,287 --> 00:08:20,818
Livin' the dream.

204
00:08:21,144 --> 00:08:23,829
Mm. Oh, damn it.
I forgot to punish Brick.

205
00:08:23,990 --> 00:08:26,578
He didn't lick another mailbox
again, did he?

206
00:08:26,699 --> 00:08:29,453
No, but after the game
yesterday, he was a real jerk.

207
00:08:29,576 --> 00:08:31,058
I didn't want to make a scene
in front of the guys,

208
00:08:31,203 --> 00:08:32,376
but I was pretty pissed.

209
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:34,679
Then I had a couple beers
and I forgot about it.

210
00:08:34,835 --> 00:08:36,245
So how's the job search coming?

211
00:08:36,426 --> 00:08:39,432
Well, I spent the whole morning
looking through the want ads,

212
00:08:39,544 --> 00:08:40,904
and it got me to thinking.

213
00:08:41,086 --> 00:08:44,858
Why should I go out
and get another crappy job?

214
00:08:45,437 --> 00:08:47,386
'Cause you lost
your old crappy job?

215
00:08:47,505 --> 00:08:48,852
No, I'm serious, Mike.

216
00:08:49,038 --> 00:08:51,585
I mean, I'm sitting there,
and it dawned on me.

217
00:08:51,686 --> 00:08:54,350
I never think long-term
about my life.

218
00:08:54,462 --> 00:08:56,072
I always take the first thing
that comes along--

219
00:08:56,182 --> 00:08:57,666
first job, first house,

220
00:08:57,770 --> 00:08:59,445
first guy that asked me
to marry him.

221
00:08:59,572 --> 00:09:00,330
Mm-hmm.

222
00:09:00,453 --> 00:09:02,394
Let's face it, Mike.
We're eeny people.

223
00:09:03,369 --> 00:09:05,836
You know, eeny meeny miny mo.

224
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:07,582
Just that we never make it
to "mo."

225
00:09:07,753 --> 00:09:08,989
We always stop at "eeny."

226
00:09:09,095 --> 00:09:11,610
Eeny houses, eeny cars,
eeny kids.

227
00:09:11,735 --> 00:09:13,850
Don't you ever just wanna
be a "mo" person?

228
00:09:13,979 --> 00:09:16,351
You know, sometimes, if you just
open the paper and point,

229
00:09:16,527 --> 00:09:17,838
you can find
a pretty great job.

230
00:09:17,954 --> 00:09:19,368
Eeny thinking!

231
00:09:19,633 --> 00:09:22,140
I don't want to just take
the next random job

232
00:09:22,317 --> 00:09:23,350
like I always do.

233
00:09:23,457 --> 00:09:25,655
I wanna do something
that I'm passionate about.

234
00:09:25,793 --> 00:09:27,964
Oh, no. You're not gonna start
beading again, are ya?

235
00:09:28,095 --> 00:09:29,447
No. That was dumb.

236
00:09:29,595 --> 00:09:30,655
Look, I'm just talking about

237
00:09:30,785 --> 00:09:32,963
finding something
that I'm good at,

238
00:09:33,136 --> 00:09:35,876
maybe going back to school
and...

239
00:09:36,022 --> 00:09:36,953
I don't know.

240
00:09:37,081 --> 00:09:39,534
Learning a skill that people
might wanna pay me for.

241
00:09:39,672 --> 00:09:41,337
You know,
I read in "More" magazine

242
00:09:41,460 --> 00:09:44,728
there's lots of people who have
these whole second acts.

243
00:09:44,906 --> 00:09:46,148
How old are these people?

244
00:09:46,262 --> 00:09:48,177
Old, Mike. Like us.

245
00:09:48,312 --> 00:09:50,634
What do you think?
Second act, huh?

246
00:09:50,799 --> 00:09:52,402
If it doesn't work out,
I promise I will take

247
00:09:52,539 --> 00:09:56,201
the next low-paying, demeaning
crap job that comes my way.

248
00:09:58,410 --> 00:09:59,031
Mm.

249
00:09:59,137 --> 00:10:00,505
Man, I'm beat.

250
00:10:01,239 --> 00:10:03,073
Hey, hold on a sec.
I wanna talk to you.

251
00:10:03,190 --> 00:10:04,552
I got picked on
by a third grader,

252
00:10:04,667 --> 00:10:05,714
but when I told my teacher,

253
00:10:05,853 --> 00:10:08,469
he said since I'm in fifth now,
I should just handle it.

254
00:10:08,622 --> 00:10:10,660
So I went back to reason
with the third grader,

255
00:10:10,819 --> 00:10:13,079
and he took my lunch,
pushed me down,

256
00:10:13,203 --> 00:10:15,261
and had the rest of
the third graders stand on me.

257
00:10:15,951 --> 00:10:18,216
Sorry. What did you want
to talk to me about again?

258
00:10:19,128 --> 00:10:20,351
Uh...

259
00:10:20,466 --> 00:10:21,797
I'll, uh, catch you later.

260
00:10:25,654 --> 00:10:27,802
So your dad and I have
some news.

261
00:10:27,923 --> 00:10:30,398
Yes! I'm gettin' a new car!
Whoo!

262
00:10:30,513 --> 00:10:31,058
What?

263
00:10:31,178 --> 00:10:32,433
I've totally been eavesdropping
on you guys,

264
00:10:32,557 --> 00:10:35,452
and I overheard mom talking
about a "meeting with Ehlert"

265
00:10:35,569 --> 00:10:36,808
and "it's time
for a fresh start."

266
00:10:36,912 --> 00:10:39,770
And I'm gettin' a new car!
In your faces! Seniors rule!

267
00:10:39,903 --> 00:10:41,350
Continue.

268
00:10:41,524 --> 00:10:42,873
You're not getting a new car.

269
00:10:42,997 --> 00:10:44,379
Your mom lost her job.

270
00:10:44,494 --> 00:10:45,376
You got fired?

271
00:10:45,477 --> 00:10:47,891
- No, I wasn't fired. I was let go.
- Oh, no.

272
00:10:48,031 --> 00:10:49,225
Are we gonna lose the house?

273
00:10:49,348 --> 00:10:51,193
I can't go to Hickory Farms.
<i>I can't.</i>

274
00:10:51,228 --> 00:10:54,270
It's Hickory <i>Arms,</i>
and we're not moving anywhere.

275
00:10:54,434 --> 00:10:58,266
We decided that instead of me
getting another random job,

276
00:10:59,054 --> 00:11:02,536
I'm gonna go back to school
and pursue an actual career.

277
00:11:02,719 --> 00:11:03,734
But that means
in the short-term,

278
00:11:03,861 --> 00:11:05,720
we're all gonna have to tighten
our belts around here.

279
00:11:05,901 --> 00:11:08,778
<i>Oh,</i> my God. I thought you were
getting me a car.

280
00:11:08,888 --> 00:11:10,582
Now you're saying
we're gonna be even poorer?

281
00:11:10,710 --> 00:11:12,212
I can't believe this!

282
00:11:12,331 --> 00:11:14,261
I was so nice to you guys
all summer!

283
00:11:14,378 --> 00:11:16,609
I barely even yelled at you,
and this is how you thank me?!

284
00:11:16,723 --> 00:11:17,851
A teacher at my school got fired

285
00:11:17,971 --> 00:11:19,898
because she had
an inappropriate relationship.

286
00:11:20,036 --> 00:11:21,763
Did you have
an inappropriate relationship?

287
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:25,225
<i>No.</i> The point is,
your dad and I have decided

288
00:11:25,359 --> 00:11:28,154
that it's not too late for me
to have a second act.

289
00:11:28,274 --> 00:11:29,785
Oh, no. Is she gonna start
beading again?

290
00:11:29,897 --> 00:11:31,505
No. Your mom agreed
that was dumb.

291
00:11:31,624 --> 00:11:33,877
It's not dumb.
I love mom's beading.

292
00:11:33,988 --> 00:11:35,274
She made me this necklace.

293
00:11:37,307 --> 00:11:39,239
Oh, this is so unfair!

294
00:11:39,509 --> 00:11:40,819
It's my senior year.

295
00:11:40,929 --> 00:11:42,573
How am I supposed to rule
without a car?!

296
00:11:42,713 --> 00:11:44,254
Well, your mom's going
back to school.

297
00:11:44,356 --> 00:11:45,139
If you want a new car,

298
00:11:45,243 --> 00:11:46,787
you'll have to get a second job
to pay for it.

299
00:11:46,877 --> 00:11:48,705
So I'm gonna have two jobs.
She's gonna have none?

300
00:11:48,879 --> 00:11:49,881
You're the mom!

301
00:11:50,003 --> 00:11:52,165
You're the one who's supposed to
be working yourself to death!

302
00:11:52,575 --> 00:11:53,508
Wait.

303
00:11:54,691 --> 00:11:57,069
You guys are messing with me,
right? This is all a big setup.

304
00:11:57,176 --> 00:11:58,677
Is my new car
outside the window?

305
00:12:00,326 --> 00:12:02,548
You're not getting a car,
you idiot.

306
00:12:02,686 --> 00:12:04,171
We'll be lucky if we don't
have to sell one of ours

307
00:12:04,302 --> 00:12:05,543
to pay for mom's school.

308
00:12:05,663 --> 00:12:07,803
What? I can't believe
you're telling me this!

309
00:12:07,916 --> 00:12:09,224
These are adult problems.

310
00:12:09,357 --> 00:12:10,964
Whatever happened
to protecting our feelings?

311
00:12:11,073 --> 00:12:12,623
Whatever happened to
not upsetting the kids?

312
00:12:12,737 --> 00:12:14,807
We thought you'd be
mature enough to handle it.

313
00:12:14,945 --> 00:12:16,489
Well, I'm not.
So from now on,

314
00:12:16,607 --> 00:12:18,435
don't be sitting us down
and telling us bad things.

315
00:12:18,541 --> 00:12:20,308
We'll just assume
those are happening.

316
00:12:20,432 --> 00:12:23,215
Stop whining, Axl. It's called
being part of a family,

317
00:12:23,334 --> 00:12:25,391
so we're gonna handle this
as a family.

318
00:12:25,501 --> 00:12:26,697
And where the hell
did Brick go?

319
00:12:26,816 --> 00:12:28,998
Brick, get back in here!
I still gotta talk to--

320
00:12:29,098 --> 00:12:30,151
this is my gap year fund,

321
00:12:30,286 --> 00:12:31,944
but you can have it
if it helps out.

322
00:12:32,364 --> 00:12:33,187
See?

323
00:12:33,295 --> 00:12:34,959
Now this is a good brother.

324
00:12:35,090 --> 00:12:36,423
He's chipping in
for my new car.

325
00:12:48,106 --> 00:12:49,325
What's that?

326
00:12:49,433 --> 00:12:50,486
Powdered milk.

327
00:12:50,586 --> 00:12:52,570
It'll last us, like, nine months
and save us a bundle.

328
00:12:52,675 --> 00:12:55,054
Help me unload the car and I'll
scoop you a nice tall glass.

329
00:12:55,180 --> 00:12:57,004
Mom, dad, what would you do

330
00:12:57,143 --> 00:12:59,439
if you knew someone who wanted
to run for something at school

331
00:12:59,627 --> 00:13:01,963
but you <i>know</i> it's
a real long shot?

332
00:13:02,085 --> 00:13:03,862
Oh, Sue.
What are you trying out for now?

333
00:13:03,999 --> 00:13:05,916
- Well, homecoming court, but--
- You're never gonna make that.

334
00:13:06,089 --> 00:13:07,791
Mike! Don't tell her that.

335
00:13:07,934 --> 00:13:10,279
Why, mom?
You think I <i>could</i> make it?

336
00:13:10,418 --> 00:13:13,497
Well, it sounds like your dad
already said "no," so...

337
00:13:13,689 --> 00:13:15,335
Oh, well, it doesn't matter.
It's not me.

338
00:13:15,489 --> 00:13:17,028
It's my mentee, Jenna.

339
00:13:17,182 --> 00:13:18,876
She's doing really well
in school,

340
00:13:19,043 --> 00:13:20,913
but homecoming court
is a whole other thing.

341
00:13:21,043 --> 00:13:23,335
They only pick
the most fabulous girls

342
00:13:23,451 --> 00:13:25,298
with the straightest hair
and the whitest teeth.

343
00:13:25,428 --> 00:13:27,532
It's almost like
it's a popularity contest.

344
00:13:27,685 --> 00:13:29,832
But what do you say to someone
who wants to try for something

345
00:13:29,975 --> 00:13:32,234
that is so far
out of their reach?

346
00:13:32,521 --> 00:13:34,067
Oh. That's a tough one.

347
00:13:34,201 --> 00:13:38,931
Um, but off the top of my head,
I would look her in the eye

348
00:13:39,122 --> 00:13:42,658
and say, "Sue"--
or whatever her name is--

349
00:13:42,824 --> 00:13:46,087
"I really admire your spirit
and tenacity.

350
00:13:46,212 --> 00:13:49,366
And whatever happens,
I'm here for you."

351
00:13:49,499 --> 00:13:51,467
That is spot-on advice.

352
00:13:51,628 --> 00:13:55,284
You should guest blog
for kickinitteenstyle.com.

353
00:13:58,283 --> 00:13:59,775
Are you sure we didn't just
encourage Sue

354
00:13:59,890 --> 00:14:01,195
to try out
for homecoming court?

355
00:14:01,306 --> 00:14:02,455
No, it's not her.

356
00:14:02,587 --> 00:14:04,080
- You sure?
- I mean...

357
00:14:04,198 --> 00:14:06,716
I think so, but I was
kind of half-listening.

358
00:14:06,883 --> 00:14:08,126
- Me, too.
- What?

359
00:14:08,251 --> 00:14:09,474
What?

360
00:14:10,353 --> 00:14:11,289
<i>So the next day,</i>

361
00:14:11,416 --> 00:14:14,434
<i>I was off to enroll at</i>
<i>the Orson Technical Institute,</i>

362
00:14:14,577 --> 00:14:16,542
<i>where second acts are born.</i>

363
00:14:17,091 --> 00:14:18,515
Can
we please get rid of this milk?

364
00:14:18,625 --> 00:14:19,939
I feel like I'm drinking chalk.

365
00:14:20,058 --> 00:14:21,907
Did you stir it? I told you,
you have to stir it.

366
00:14:22,070 --> 00:14:24,747
Yeah, I tried.
It just goes back to milk dust.

367
00:14:24,869 --> 00:14:26,396
No, no,
<i>while</i> you're drinking it.

368
00:14:26,557 --> 00:14:28,672
- Look, here. Yeah.
- Huh?

369
00:14:28,812 --> 00:14:31,314
- Stir and drink. Stir and drink.
- Stir and...

370
00:14:31,671 --> 00:14:33,763
Well, it'll be good on cereal.

371
00:14:34,172 --> 00:14:34,765
Mm.

372
00:14:34,876 --> 00:14:37,234
Ooh, it's a commercial
for my school! Turn it up.

373
00:14:37,383 --> 00:14:39,401
<i>Do you dream of a
career in the exciting field</i>

374
00:14:39,524 --> 00:14:40,730
<i>of air conditioning repair</i>

375
00:14:40,853 --> 00:14:43,115
<i>or health information</i>
<i>technology?</i>

376
00:14:43,270 --> 00:14:46,363
<i>I did, and now I'm a certified</i>
<i>occupational therapist.</i>

377
00:14:46,508 --> 00:14:47,904
Ooh, an "-ist"!

378
00:14:48,013 --> 00:14:49,683
I've always wanted to be
an "-ist."

379
00:14:49,802 --> 00:14:51,153
<i>In just a few short months,</i>

380
00:14:51,249 --> 00:14:53,588
<i>I learned the skills I needed</i>
<i>to become a court reporter.</i>

381
00:14:53,705 --> 00:14:55,192
I could be a court reporter.

382
00:14:55,369 --> 00:14:56,853
Whenever I watch
those crime shows,

383
00:14:56,994 --> 00:14:59,857
I always know who the criminal
is before they reveal it.

384
00:15:00,088 --> 00:15:02,314
I don't think you'd actually
be solving crimes.

385
00:15:02,437 --> 00:15:03,438
No, no, I'd just be assisting.

386
00:15:03,565 --> 00:15:05,937
But the cops would come
to rely on me for my expertise.

387
00:15:06,075 --> 00:15:07,602
<i>The jobs of tomorrow</i>
<i>are here today</i>

388
00:15:07,771 --> 00:15:09,519
<i>in our cutting-edge</i>
<i>computer department.</i>

389
00:15:09,717 --> 00:15:12,428
Oh, that might be good.
I'm handy with computers.

390
00:15:14,516 --> 00:15:16,948
Hey! Whenever you try
to watch a video online,

391
00:15:17,092 --> 00:15:18,030
I'm always the one

392
00:15:18,152 --> 00:15:19,402
that shows you how
to make the screen bigger.

393
00:15:19,541 --> 00:15:21,213
- You <i>never</i> know how.
<i>- Thanks, OTI!</i>

394
00:15:21,291 --> 00:15:23,106
I'm not gonna let you step
on my buzz, Mike.

395
00:15:23,252 --> 00:15:24,461
Hmm? Starting now,

396
00:15:24,604 --> 00:15:27,445
I'm gonna stop dreaming
it and start living it.

397
00:15:30,357 --> 00:15:31,447
You need to settle down

398
00:15:31,564 --> 00:15:33,552
if we're gonna announce
the homecoming court.

399
00:15:34,915 --> 00:15:36,593
I'm not kidding, people.

400
00:15:36,775 --> 00:15:38,369
Fine. I'll wait.

401
00:15:38,478 --> 00:15:40,307
I am so worried for her.

402
00:15:40,458 --> 00:15:42,199
I don't know if I should
go over there or not.

403
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:44,595
I'm not even talking
to my mentee.

404
00:15:44,697 --> 00:15:45,989
I don't want to say
what happened,

405
00:15:46,090 --> 00:15:48,153
but there was a gift of a signed
"Jersey Boys" "Playbill"

406
00:15:48,264 --> 00:15:49,881
that was not
graciously received.

407
00:15:50,004 --> 00:15:51,617
All right, seriously, people.

408
00:15:51,775 --> 00:15:54,301
Okay, the votes
have been tallied,

409
00:15:54,495 --> 00:15:56,945
and your homecoming court is
as follows.

410
00:15:57,061 --> 00:16:00,873
For the seniors,
Caroline Douglas and Axl Heck.

411
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:02,251
Yes!

412
00:16:02,358 --> 00:16:04,185
- For the juniors--
- Real quick.

413
00:16:04,301 --> 00:16:06,099
Uh, you may have heard I totaled
my car in a demolition derby.

414
00:16:06,213 --> 00:16:07,381
Not to worry, ladies.

415
00:16:07,515 --> 00:16:10,909
- I will have another sweet ride by homecoming.
- Seniors rule!

416
00:16:11,085 --> 00:16:12,566
Whoo!

417
00:16:13,986 --> 00:16:17,110
Juniors--
Kurt Kellogg, Amy Slaughter.

418
00:16:18,865 --> 00:16:21,836
Sophomores--
Chris Aillo, Courtney Sheehan.

419
00:16:23,141 --> 00:16:25,459
And finally,
for the freshmen class--

420
00:16:26,092 --> 00:16:28,511
Ben Anderson and...

421
00:16:28,614 --> 00:16:30,104
Jenna Taylor!

422
00:16:32,648 --> 00:16:35,126
Oh, my <i>God.</i>

423
00:16:36,053 --> 00:16:38,581
We made homecoming court!

424
00:16:38,696 --> 00:16:39,776
Aah!

425
00:16:48,078 --> 00:16:49,582
Brick, I need to talk to you.

426
00:16:50,387 --> 00:16:51,841
<i>Now.</i>

427
00:16:52,349 --> 00:16:53,595
Go.

428
00:16:53,710 --> 00:16:54,720
I wanted you to know

429
00:16:54,825 --> 00:16:56,405
that what you did
at the sub shop the other day

430
00:16:56,506 --> 00:16:57,640
was not okay.

431
00:16:57,858 --> 00:17:00,314
And, uh, I'm not gonna punish
you anymore at this point

432
00:17:00,473 --> 00:17:02,139
'cause time has passed.

433
00:17:02,257 --> 00:17:05,742
But you need to understand that
you were rude and disrespectful.

434
00:17:06,248 --> 00:17:08,043
W-what'd I do again?

435
00:17:08,190 --> 00:17:09,819
You ignored me
and the Morrisons.

436
00:17:10,085 --> 00:17:12,807
Okay. But in my defense,
I was at the end of a book.

437
00:17:12,926 --> 00:17:14,264
That doesn't make a difference.

438
00:17:14,410 --> 00:17:16,025
Mm, not to you.
You're not a big reader.

439
00:17:16,128 --> 00:17:17,577
Look, I'm not punishing you,

440
00:17:17,689 --> 00:17:19,685
but you can never
talk to me like that again,

441
00:17:19,808 --> 00:17:21,611
especially in public
with other people around.

442
00:17:21,728 --> 00:17:22,722
It's embarrassing.

443
00:17:22,838 --> 00:17:23,640
Really?

444
00:17:23,730 --> 00:17:25,065
But you guys weren't
embarrassed to be, like, 50

445
00:17:25,168 --> 00:17:26,932
and walk-running
around those bases?

446
00:17:27,061 --> 00:17:28,676
Okay,

447
00:17:28,822 --> 00:17:31,222
I'm trying to let you
off the hook here,

448
00:17:31,369 --> 00:17:33,808
but you need to get that
you did something wrong.

449
00:17:33,926 --> 00:17:35,918
You ignored me
and the Morrisons three times.

450
00:17:36,040 --> 00:17:36,952
N-not three times,

451
00:17:37,092 --> 00:17:38,775
'cause the first time
I didn't even hear you.

452
00:17:38,895 --> 00:17:40,689
<i>Whatever.</i>
You ignored me twice.

453
00:17:40,896 --> 00:17:42,694
So I'm being punished
for ignoring you twice?

454
00:17:42,830 --> 00:17:44,405
I told you,
I'm <i>not</i> punishing you.

455
00:17:44,520 --> 00:17:45,371
'Cause I'm just saying,

456
00:17:45,493 --> 00:17:47,456
when you're reading the sports
page, and I try to talk to you,

457
00:17:47,581 --> 00:17:49,335
you tell me I'm being rude
for interrupting.

458
00:17:49,452 --> 00:17:51,898
But when someone interrupts
<i>my</i> reading, I get punished.

459
00:17:52,011 --> 00:17:53,304
I'm not punishing you!

460
00:17:53,507 --> 00:17:55,407
I just need some clarification.

461
00:17:55,572 --> 00:17:57,469
You want some clarification?
Fine.

462
00:17:57,595 --> 00:17:59,779
You're punished. Two weeks!

463
00:18:00,216 --> 00:18:01,516
Two weeks without what?

464
00:18:01,623 --> 00:18:03,226
I'll get back to ya!

465
00:18:04,361 --> 00:18:05,517
Mom!

466
00:18:06,027 --> 00:18:07,140
These chips are stale.

467
00:18:07,354 --> 00:18:09,546
I'm never gonna
amount to anything!

468
00:18:09,656 --> 00:18:12,155
I'm a big, fat failure!

469
00:18:12,637 --> 00:18:14,020
You know what?

470
00:18:14,137 --> 00:18:15,149
I like 'em.

471
00:18:15,274 --> 00:18:16,404
I don't know what
I was thinking.

472
00:18:16,514 --> 00:18:18,138
I can't go back to school.

473
00:18:18,270 --> 00:18:19,514
I like 'em chewy.

474
00:18:19,674 --> 00:18:21,266
They're like gum.
They're like chip gum.

475
00:18:21,439 --> 00:18:24,084
I went down to Orson Tech,
and I was all excited.

476
00:18:24,227 --> 00:18:27,267
And then the lady started
asking me all these questions.

477
00:18:27,390 --> 00:18:29,405
And I just got so overwhelmed!

478
00:18:29,506 --> 00:18:31,966
Dad? Sue? Brick?

479
00:18:32,070 --> 00:18:33,066
I don't know what
I was thinking.

480
00:18:33,186 --> 00:18:34,912
I don't know computers!

481
00:18:35,174 --> 00:18:36,870
I mean, I know how
to make the screen big,

482
00:18:36,989 --> 00:18:38,353
but I can't
make it small again!

483
00:18:38,443 --> 00:18:40,278
I just unplug the computer!

484
00:18:40,425 --> 00:18:43,106
And the only reason I can
figure out those crime shows

485
00:18:43,235 --> 00:18:46,317
is because it's always
the big guest star who's guilty.

486
00:18:46,433 --> 00:18:50,533
Of course Jeff Goldblum
strangled the piano tutor! Duh!

487
00:18:51,022 --> 00:18:52,387
But...

488
00:18:53,411 --> 00:18:54,650
You don't need
to know all this now.

489
00:18:54,743 --> 00:18:57,165
That's what school's for.
You'll, like, study it.

490
00:18:57,556 --> 00:18:59,821
I haven't studied in 20 years.

491
00:18:59,935 --> 00:19:01,328
I mean, look at this.

492
00:19:01,461 --> 00:19:05,990
Every course requires, like,
tons of reading every night.

493
00:19:06,131 --> 00:19:09,930
And I know this sounds petty,
but the exams are on Tuesday,

494
00:19:10,029 --> 00:19:12,491
and The Bachelor's
on Monday night!

495
00:19:12,811 --> 00:19:14,003
Look, it's like you always
tell me.

496
00:19:15,458 --> 00:19:19,385
You gotta budget your time,
apply yourself, and focus.

497
00:19:19,630 --> 00:19:20,889
You're young.

498
00:19:20,999 --> 00:19:22,512
You have your whole life
ahead of you.

499
00:19:22,667 --> 00:19:26,209
I don't know why I thought
I could have a second act.

500
00:19:26,471 --> 00:19:29,793
I just have this whole long
crappy first act,

501
00:19:29,924 --> 00:19:32,472
and at the intermission,
nobody <i>cares.</i>

502
00:19:32,587 --> 00:19:34,135
Come on.
You're not that old.

503
00:19:34,286 --> 00:19:36,305
Darrin saw you going to get
the mail the other day.

504
00:19:36,433 --> 00:19:38,768
He said you looked pretty good
for a woman your age.

505
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:40,935
Really? He said that?

506
00:19:41,077 --> 00:19:44,932
Are you sure? Like,
what were his exact words?

507
00:19:45,040 --> 00:19:46,422
'Cause sometimes you say
a little bit,

508
00:19:46,524 --> 00:19:48,480
but it's not the whole
conversation.

509
00:19:48,724 --> 00:19:50,091
Mom, you can do this.

510
00:19:50,321 --> 00:19:52,770
If you look hard enough, you can
find that straight A student.

511
00:19:52,873 --> 00:19:53,884
And when you do,

512
00:19:54,021 --> 00:19:56,301
try to sit right next to them
so you can copy off their test.

513
00:19:58,005 --> 00:20:00,628
I don't know. Maybe I can.

514
00:20:00,725 --> 00:20:02,507
But even if could, like,

515
00:20:02,653 --> 00:20:05,368
how am I gonna study
or do homework?

516
00:20:05,484 --> 00:20:08,344
It's not like the laundry's
gonna stop coming.

517
00:20:08,550 --> 00:20:09,931
Mom...

518
00:20:10,281 --> 00:20:12,310
You don't need to do this
all alone.

519
00:20:14,040 --> 00:20:15,681
Really?

520
00:20:15,946 --> 00:20:17,023
Yeah.

521
00:20:17,626 --> 00:20:18,938
It's about time
dad, Sue, and Brick

522
00:20:19,056 --> 00:20:20,800
started pulling their weight
around here.

523
00:20:22,007 --> 00:20:23,209
Yeah.

524
00:20:29,589 --> 00:20:30,906
Hey, Sue. Wanna sit down?

525
00:20:31,013 --> 00:20:32,167
No, Jenna.

526
00:20:32,319 --> 00:20:34,268
Actually, I just wanted
to let you know

527
00:20:34,375 --> 00:20:36,121
that I think my work here
is done.

528
00:20:36,256 --> 00:20:39,387
Under my tutelage, you have
become a cheerleader,

529
00:20:39,515 --> 00:20:41,714
landed a really stunning
boyfriend,

530
00:20:41,858 --> 00:20:43,872
and been elected
to homecoming court.

531
00:20:44,013 --> 00:20:46,454
But now it is time for you
to fly on your own.

532
00:20:46,648 --> 00:20:48,205
Plus, it's really not cool

533
00:20:48,315 --> 00:20:50,442
for me to be sitting
at the freshmen table.

534
00:20:56,682 --> 00:20:59,343
<i>Everyone needs</i>
<i>someone to watch their back,</i>

535
00:20:59,583 --> 00:21:01,496
<i>whether you're starting</i>
<i>high school...</i>

536
00:21:02,654 --> 00:21:04,880
<i>or the second act of your life.</i>

537
00:21:06,838 --> 00:21:08,466
So how's this thing work again?

538
00:21:09,555 --> 00:21:10,640
Okay.

539
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:12,244
Well...

540
00:21:12,362 --> 00:21:13,841
First, you put in
the detergent.

541
00:21:15,047 --> 00:21:15,714
Like that.

542
00:21:15,872 --> 00:21:16,839
Then...

543
00:21:17,169 --> 00:21:18,742
You get a new piece
of duct tape,

544
00:21:18,935 --> 00:21:20,726
put it over the lid,

545
00:21:22,052 --> 00:21:23,211
turn the dial--

546
00:21:23,324 --> 00:21:26,255
oh, and remember,
it's broken, so "hot" is cold.

547
00:21:26,393 --> 00:21:28,143
But "cold" is also cold...

548
00:21:28,229 --> 00:21:29,708
But use hot cold
instead of cold cold,

549
00:21:29,808 --> 00:21:31,593
'cause cold cold uses
too much water.

550
00:21:31,708 --> 00:21:33,245
And sometimes it gets
a little wobbly,

551
00:21:33,346 --> 00:21:34,773
and it might get out of balance,
so if you just...

552
00:21:34,914 --> 00:21:36,225
give it a hip check.

553
00:21:36,344 --> 00:21:37,329
Yeah. Go ahead.

554
00:21:39,829 --> 00:21:42,782
Sync and corrections by n17t01
www.addic7ed.com

